add('practical-thinking', `
0 | Start with the difference between a familiar routine and a reason for it. This establishes the object of practical thinking: relations that actually matter to a task. | The workshop case and preserved first entry provide a strong course spine. Explain its connection to Steiner's practical training passage before beginning the journal. | State a routine's aim, its assumed reason and an observation that would challenge that reason.
1 | Inquiry requires noticing how liking our own idea affects attention. This prepares fair observation. | Keep the inconvenient-feedback case. Three sessions need not each be a separate day; offer a manageable schedule so repetition has a purpose rather than becoming administration. | Record a welcome and unwelcome observation and examine both by the same standard.
2 | Observation supplies material for thinking before an explanation is added. The sign case continues the opening question. | Strong fit, but changing immediately to a cup or plant can weaken continuity. Offer 'continue the workshop' as the default, with a personal object as an alternative. | Separate a visible pause from the inference that someone was confused.
3 | Successive observations introduce change and missing information. This advances beyond a single description. | Continue the same object. Have learners mark what changed in the object and what changed in their attention, rather than invent a cause for every difference. | Describe a change and two possible explanations, identifying what would distinguish them.
4 | A prediction makes thinking answerable to an outcome. It follows observation of change. | The relocated sign fits very well. Include a sample where the prediction fails and show how that can improve understanding. | Compare expectation and result, then revise a reason rather than simply record success/failure.
5 | Reconstructing a sequence reveals dependencies, rather than just remembering the result. | The workshop preparation time is clear. Add the conceptual bridge to active thinking: the learner supplies and examines the connection between steps. | Explain why one step depends on another and adjust the sequence when a condition changes.
6 | Sustained thought develops the ability to stay with a chosen relationship instead of following every association. | The short session fits. Provide one example of a connected chain and one of a merely associated list; returning is the skill, not uninterrupted perfection. | Identify a diversion and explain the relationship used to return to the question.
7 | A checked mental picture distinguishes retained observation from plausible invention. This complements sustained thought with accuracy. | Strong exercise with direct feedback. Offer spoken or written reconstruction equally, so drawing skill is not mistaken for success. | Identify one confidently invented detail and improve the next observation strategy.
8 | A conclusion integrates observation, prediction and alternative explanations. It should be proportionate to what was learned. | Reusing earlier predictions gives this lesson real continuity. Keep multiple changes visible instead of implying the sign's location explains everything. | Choose a reversible next step, explain the evidence and name a reason to reconsider.
9 | Review turns the earlier records into evidence of changed thinking. The goal is a usable habit, not journal volume. | One of the strongest endings. Reduce the mandatory extra three sessions to an optional follow-up chosen from the learner's actual difficulty. | Show two specific before/after changes and select one justified practice to continue.
`);

add('philosophy-of-freedom', `
0 | The book has two connected questions: knowing and acting freely. Orientation should make that architecture visible. | The disputed meeting time introduces checking knowledge. Add a decision about the same meeting so one case can develop through both halves of the course. | State the difference between 'How do I know?' and 'Why do I act?' in the continuing case.
1 | Having options does not settle the origin of a motive. This is the problem the later theory of freedom must answer. | The invitation case fits. Preserve the first account of the motive for the final lesson; do not ask learners to certify themselves as free. | Distinguish options, ability to act and understood motive.
2 | Seeking explanations exposes the relation between world and knowing subject. This motivates the inquiry into thinking. | Observation-versus-inference is useful, but the lesson must also explain why Steiner turns the question back onto the knower. Add that final connecting sentence. | Explain what the observer contributes to forming a question, beyond listing possible causes.
4 | Thinking makes conceptual connections and can subsequently examine its own activity. This is a central argumentative step. | Arithmetic is well suited to experiencing a connection. Follow it immediately with Steiner's claim about observing thinking and a question about that claim. | Distinguish the calculation, recalling its answer and retrospectively examining the thinking.
5 | Perception, representation and the argument that we know only representations must be separated. This prepares the account of knowledge. | The tilted card is useful preparation, but the philosophical argument is harder than the visual example. Supply a worked three-step argument and its disputed assumption. | Reconstruct the objection accurately without treating it as automatic proof of the opposite position.
7 | Knowing brings percept and concept into relation. The flower's successive appearances make the need for a conceptual connection visible. | Strong central example, but the triangle adds another task. Teach the flower first and make the shared-concept comparison a short second step or optional deepening. | Explain why a list of appearances is not yet an account of development.
9 | A representation individualizes conceptual content through a particular encounter; feeling adds personal significance. | The bicycle case closely matches the concepts. Keep the distinction between recognizing another bicycle and remembering that bicycle. | Separate general concept, remembered encounter and feeling without making truth merely private.
10 | Limits of knowledge require more than pointing to a present information gap. This closes a major question in the first half. | The attendance example clarifies a limited point. It cannot replace the monism argument: add a diagram of the explanatory separation Steiner criticizes. | Distinguish unavailable information from a claimed principled barrier, then reconstruct the source's argument.
11 | Thinking, feeling and willing connect knowing to action. This is the bridge into the second half, rather than a new personality classification. | The chair example fits. Explicitly connect it back to percept/concept and forward to motive; otherwise it repeats the introductory threefold exercise. | Explain how understanding a situation, its personal significance and acting relate without being isolated stages.
12 | Ethical individualism asks when an understood ethical content becomes the ground of a deed. It develops the opening motive question. | The missed-meeting response is suitable. Provide an annotated distinction between aim, driving force and reason before mixed motives complicate the case. | Compare outwardly identical actions with different motivating grounds and identify what remains uncertain.
14 | A shared world of ideas need not produce identical actions. This locates individual freedom within social life. | Summary-versus-conversation is a good comparison. Add the source's relation to authority and monism; agreement alone is not the philosophical explanation. | Explain how shared understanding can allow different fitting deeds.
15 | Purpose is a represented future aim acting through present activity. It is distinct from a cause acting backward in time. | The meeting and cancellation diagram makes the concept clear. Briefly compare a purposeful human action with a natural development to restore the chapter's wider question. | Explain how a cancelled meeting can still have guided purposeful preparation.
16 | Moral intuition, imagination and technique connect an ethical insight with an appropriate and workable deed. | This is a particularly strong application lesson. Keep the new audio preference and explain why changing the implementation need not change the ethical reason. Move the separate evolution argument into a labelled reading step. | Revise a proposal and practical means in response to the person while explaining what happens to its guiding reason.
18 | Desire, enjoyment and the value of a particular aim are different. This examines a competing account of motivation and life's value. | The music example fits. Add the specific pessimistic argument being answered, so the lesson is not simply advice to persevere. | Explain why an unrelated pleasure does not necessarily satisfy the chosen aim and when revision is reasonable.
20 | Understanding an individual requires attention to their reasons beyond a category. This gives ethical individualism a social consequence. | Strong everyday application. Explicitly reconnect to the individuality and motive lessons, then apply it to the use of temperament descriptions. | Correct an inference using the person's account and explain why even a flattering stereotype can obstruct understanding.
21 | The synthesis must show how the account of thinking supports the account of free action. This is the book's overall logic. | Good ambition, but many checklist items obscure the central argument. Start with a five-link map, then assess the opening case and one objection. | Explain the knowledge-to-freedom connection using passages from both halves, without inferring freedom from a successful result.
3 | Optional deepening of the thinking lesson through event and conceptual explanation. | Retain as an alternative example or targeted help after file 4; do not make the cup exercise another mandatory pass through the same distinction. | Explain what conceptual connection adds without saying thought physically caused the event.
6 | Optional close reading of the representations argument already included in file 5. | Use when a learner cannot yet reconstruct the argument. Label the precise difficulty it helps resolve. | Identify the disputed premise and the limited scope of Steiner's objection.
8 | Optional clarification of shared concepts versus individual experience, supporting file 7. | The triangle exercise earns its place as focused help. Avoid duplicating the same memory/feeling worksheet for students who already understand it. | Show how different drawings can instantiate the same conceptual relation.
13 | Optional clarification of ethical individualism after file 12. | Use the alternative motives as a worked response with commentary, rather than another compulsory written assignment. | Explain why an individually grasped ethical idea is not identical to an arbitrary preference.
17 | Optional worked implementation example supporting file 16. | Fold the digital-copy example into expandable help. The main audio-preference case already teaches the same progression. | Distinguish reason, concrete invention and practical execution.
19 | Optional second example of valuing a particular aim, supporting file 18. | Retain for transfer to a different context or learners who find the music example inaccessible. Completion should not be required twice. | Explain why changing an unsafe repair plan can preserve the underlying aim.
`);

